  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ In the given interval, the excluded values are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTaking into account that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are excluded values, and arranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$",
